\subsection{What a Decryption Carries}
\label{sec:cpad}
%%
# 2026-08-29, as above. The heading of the new subsection. Appended at the end of
# Section IV so no shared section label changes number, which check_split.py
# requires.
The protocol decrypts twice. The selection step decrypts one scalar, and
each answer is key-switched to the client that asked for it. Both are intended
outputs. In an exact scheme that would end the discussion, because a decryption
returns the message and nothing else. CKKS is approximate, so a decryption
returns the message together with an error term, and that term is a function of
the secret key and of the values the circuit consumed. Li and Micciancio show
that an adversary which observes decryption results recovers the key that
produced them~\cite{limicciancio2021approximate}.
%%
# 2026-08-29, as above. States the attack and why it is a question here at all.
Their attack does not need the message. It reads the ring element
$c_0+c_1 s$ out of the decoded output and inverts it, so an argument that the
adversary cannot predict what the circuit should have produced does not answer
it. The reason the attack does not reach us is structural. It recovers whichever
key performed the decryption. Every decryption here happens after a collective
key switch that moves the ciphertext off the collective key, so the key it
recovers is the querying client's own, which that client already holds. The
collective key $s$ never appears in a relation the coalition can invert, and
$\mathsf{Enc}(\thstar)$ is never decrypted at all.
%%
# 2026-08-29, as above. The reason the attack does not reach us, stated
# structurally. It is worth being exact about this. An earlier draft argued that
# the adversary cannot know what the circuit should have produced, and that
# argument is wrong, because the attack reads the ring element out of the decoded
# output and never uses the message. The correct reason is that the key switch
# moves the ciphertext off the collective key before anyone decrypts.
The published threshold attack does not reach us either, for a separate
reason. It identifies ciphertexts whose noise is exactly zero and reads a linear
equation in the key off each one~\cite{checri2024threshold}. Detecting a
zero-noise ciphertext requires a rounding step whose success can be observed,
which exact schemes have and CKKS does not. Their statement about threshold CKKS
covers the case where no smudging is applied.
%%
# 2026-08-29, as above. Why the published threshold attack does not reach us. Its
# zero-noise identification needs an exact scheme, and its statement about
# threshold CKKS covers the case with no smudging at all.
What remains is a condition rather than an attack, and we state it because
our parameters do not meet it. The key-switch simulator of the multiparty scheme
we build on is correct when the smudging noise dominates the noise of the
ciphertext being switched, which is what lets it produce an honest client's share
without knowing that noise~\cite{mouchet2021multiparty}. That scheme sets the
requirement at $2^{\lambda/2}$ times the ciphertext noise. Our implementation
smudges at eight times the fresh-encryption noise, which is the value the library
uses in its own tests, and the serving circuit leaves a ciphertext noise several
orders of magnitude larger than that. The simulation step of
  therefore rests on a hypothesis our measured configuration
does not satisfy. What the step would otherwise hide is the circuit noise, which
depends on the honest clients' displacements. Whether that is exploitable is
open, and the scheme's authors record it as open as well.
%%
# 2026-08-29, as above. The condition we do not meet, stated as a condition and
# not as a break. This is the honest residue of the whole question. The
# multiparty scheme's own simulator assumes the smudging dominates the ciphertext
# noise, our parameters do not, and what that step would hide is the circuit
# noise rather than the key.
Meeting the stated requirement is not a matter of raising one constant.
At a plaintext scale of $2^{45}$ the required smudging exceeds the scale itself,
so it would destroy the answer rather than protect it. A configuration that
satisfies the condition needs a larger scale and modulus, and every cost in
  would move with them. We report the configuration we
measured and state the gap rather than claim a guarantee we did not instantiate.
%%
# 2026-08-29, corrected the same day. Why meeting the flooding requirement is a
# parameter change rather than a constant, and that the tension is recorded in
# the literature rather than particular to us. Threshold CKKS is insecure under
# this notion with no smudging, and flooding sized to the rule is expected to
# cost the precision of the result, so the gap is a known open tension.
Meeting the stated requirement is not a matter of raising one constant.
At a plaintext scale of $2^{45}$ the required smudging exceeds the scale itself,
so it would destroy the answer rather than protect it. A configuration that
satisfies the condition needs a larger scale and modulus, and every cost in
  would move with them. The tension is recorded in the
literature rather than particular to us. Threshold CKKS is insecure under this
notion when no smudging is applied, and flooding sized to the rule is expected
to cost the precision of the decrypted result~\cite{checri2024threshold}. We
report the configuration we measured and state the gap rather than claim a
guarantee we did not instantiate.
%%
# 2026-08-29, replacing an earlier paragraph of the same day that was wrong. The
# first version said averaging repeated queries reduces the noise until the value
# beneath it can be read off. That is false. Repetition resamples the smudging
# and not the ciphertext noise, which is fixed once the query is fixed, so the
# averaging converges on a ring element that still carries the circuit noise and
# is a hard instance at our ring degree. Repetition reaches the position of an
# implementation with no smudging, and no further.
Repeating a query bears on this and is worth stating precisely, because
the obvious reading of it is wrong. The serving circuit is deterministic, so a
client that resubmits an identical query obtains the same ciphertext while every
honest client draws fresh smudging noise. Averaging those repetitions removes the
smudging, since only the smudging is resampled. It does not remove the noise of
the ciphertext, which is fixed once the query is fixed. What the averaging
converges on is therefore the observation the smudging was hiding, and that
observation is a ring element carrying the circuit noise, which at our ring
degree is a hard instance of the underlying problem. Repetition reaches the
position of an implementation that omits smudging altogether. It does not by
itself yield a key.
%%
# 2026-08-29, as above. The control that actually matches the axis is a query
# budget with the flooding sized for it and rekeying when it is spent, at half a
# bit per doubling. Refusing to key-switch a ciphertext twice is recommended and
# supplementary, because re-randomizing a query defeats any such check.
The control that matches this is a query budget rather than a check on
duplicates. Sizing the flooding noise for $q$ decryptions costs half a bit for
each doubling of $q$, and the budget is refreshed by rekeying once it is
spent~\cite{limicciancio2022dp}. Refusing to key-switch a ciphertext twice is
cheap and we recommend it, and it is a supplementary control rather than a
substitute, because a client can re-randomize a query by adding an encryption of
zero and present a ciphertext that is fresh to any such check.
%%
# 2026-08-29. Report-only. The design paragraph is corrected against job
# 1612017, which measured the mechanism the protocol specifies for the first
# time. Two figures in the old version were wrong. The bootstrapping keys are
# 8.70 GiB and not 15.5 MiB, because the stored figure in btp_keys.json measured
# the extended secret key rather than the key set the server evaluates with. And
# the framing that the alternative is two orders of magnitude more expensive is
# dropped, because the alternative is cheaper in latency and dearer only in
# traffic.
One design decision governs the recurring figure. The argmax spends its
levels and must have them restored several times per query. Restoring them by a
collective refresh requires a share from every client for every refresh, which at
a hundred classes amounts to roughly $1.6$\,GiB of traffic for a single label.
Generating bootstrapping keys once, collectively, and letting the server restore
levels on its own reduces that traffic to zero, because evaluation under a
collectively generated key is identical to evaluation under a single key. The
protocol specifies the latter. We have now measured both, and the trade is not
one-sided.
%%
# 2026-08-29, as above. What the measurement says. Thirteen times the latency at
