%%
# 2026-08-29, Halil's notes, report view of the merged chain. Same merge of C3 and
# C4 as the submission, keeping the report's fuller explanations, and the same
# renumbering of C5 to C7 into C4 to C6.
\begin{itemize}
\item[\textbf{C1}] \emph{Optimization cannot run under encryption at acceptable
  cost.} Gradient steps, softmax, and adaptive curvature require deep, repeatedly
  bootstrapped homomorphic circuits. \emph{Therefore}, for efficiency, all learning happens in
  plaintext on the clients, and the server performs no learning.
\item[\textbf{C2}] \emph{A server computing on ciphertexts cannot read its
  inputs.} It cannot inspect, compare, or branch, so it cannot select a
  data-dependent aggregation rule. \emph{Therefore}, the choice is deferred to the
  clients, who make it without decrypting anything ( ).
\item[\textbf{C3}] \emph{Models trained from different starting points do not
  combine linearly, and multiplicative depth dominates homomorphic cost.} In the
  parameter space of a deep network they occupy incomparable regions.
  \emph{Therefore}, every client starts its trainable unit from one public
  initializer on one frozen public backbone, and the server combines what the
  clients return by a linear combination, which applies only after that common
  start. The clients carry the training and the server's work stays linear in what
  it receives, which is the cheapest operation the scheme offers and exactly the
  one the method needs.
\item[\textbf{C4}] \emph{Intermediate training signals are the strongest attack
  surface.} Each exposed round is a fresh training-time artifact, far more
  revealing than a finished model ( ). \emph{Therefore},
  the protocol is one-shot, and its single artifact is a ciphertext.
\item[\textbf{C5}] \emph{No single party may be able to decrypt.} The parties are
  mutually distrustful and the server is semi-honest. \emph{Therefore}, the secret
  key is generated and held distributively, under multiparty CKKS
  ( ).
\item[\textbf{C6}] \emph{The model is never disclosed, yet it must answer
  queries.} A query is formed from features, and the client that asks the query must be able
  to compute those features itself. \emph{Therefore}, the client runs the backbone
  and its own adapter in plaintext, and the trained quantity that is shared cannot
  sit inside the backbone.
