# 2026-08-29, Halil's two comparison points, stated plainly. slytHErin stops
# before the reduction that is 97 per cent of our query, so a label-returning
# version would pay it. CryptPEFT is two-party with the adapter in one party's
# plaintext, so it has no ciphertext-by-ciphertext product and no level
# restoration. Report-only.
CryptPEFT divides the work as we do and reaches the opposite conclusion about who
holds the secret. A public frozen backbone runs in plaintext on the client, the
client encrypts the resulting features, only the adapter is evaluated under
encryption, and the client receives the predicted label
alone~\cite{xia2026cryptpeft}. Confining encrypted computation to a small unit
after the representation is the motivation for our design as well. It is a
two-party protocol, the trained unit is the provider's plaintext, and there is no
training protocol, so it establishes that the serving arrangement is practical
without addressing how mutually distrustful parties would build the unit at all.
It reports one inference in $2.26$\,s on CIFAR-100. It is two-party and the
adapter is one party's plaintext, so it evaluates no ciphertext-by-ciphertext
product and restores no levels.
