# 2026-08-29, report view. The GPU passage loses the paragraph arguing that a
# per-primitive projection is safe, which a direct measurement of the
# restoration makes redundant, and the two sentences hedging the
# microbenchmarks. The accelerated figure is measured under Phantom on an
# H100 at the ring degree the protocol restores at. The abstract carries it,
# 48.9 to 176.6 s beside the 400.8 to 1468.3 s of one core.
\paragraph{Hardware acceleration} The figures above are single-threaded CPU, and
the argmax is the only operation slow enough to matter, so the question is what
specialised hardware does to it. Since the cost of a CKKS operation grows with the
ring degree, the comparison is only meaningful at a fixed one.  
gives our figures, $39.0$, $82.5$ and $174.9$\,ms for a ciphertext-by-ciphertext
product at ring degrees $2^{14}$, $2^{15}$ and $2^{16}$, and $33.1$, $71.6$ and
$149.7$\,ms for a rotation. Microbenchmarks of the same primitives under
Phantom, a CUDA implementation of CKKS~\cite{yang2024phantom}, at ring degree
$2^{16}$, put the product at $30$\,ms and the rotation at $29.5$\,ms, so the ratio at matched ring degree is about $5$ for
the rotation and about $6$ for the product.
